Test fixtures for the MARS. This directory, and only it, holds material
under the GNU GPL v2 (COPYING here); the rest of the repository is MIT.

imp.red and dwarf.red are the two warriors from A. K. Dewdney's 1984
Scientific American article, typed here.

validate.red (Stefan Strack, "Validate 1.1R") and rave.red come from the
pMARS 0.9.2 distribution (GNU GPL v2, see the pMARS COPYING file). They
are used as test data only and are not part of the program.

The .lst files are pMARS 0.9.2's load listings of each warrior (pmars -r 1
-c 1 file.red), produced in a gcc:13 container; battles.txt is
the oracle's result for each pair at a fixed position (pmars -r 1 -b -F pos
a.red b.red), warrior 1 at address 0 and warrior 2 at pos.

aeka.red (Kurt Franke), flashpaper.red (Matt Hastings' Flash Paper, the
macro version) and pspace.red (Stefan Strack) are also from the pMARS
0.9.2 distribution, used here for FOR/ROF, stringization and P-space;
pspace.lst is pspace.red assembled with ROUNDS = 2 and pspace12.lst with
ROUNDS = 12 (the strategy block is FOR'd in only past ten rounds).

sitter.red, pstest1.red and pstest2.red are ours: matches.txt holds their
six-round results against the sitter (pmars -r 6 -b -F 4000), which depend
only on what P-space carries from one round to the next. pMARS keeps the
-F position for the first round only, so no position-dependent pair can
serve as a multi-round fixture.
